notes               = "The heading عاشقانه‌های ابری... ترانه‌های زمستانی... is printed above this poem, but it is plural and reads like a heading for a sequence of winter love poems rather than a title for these eleven lines — page 89 is another winter ۱۳۶۸ love poem that may fall under it. Recorded as the title with that caveat; Ben to decide. The square of laughter (چهارگوش خنده‌ها here, مربع خنده‌ها in adobe-birdsong and in the Alizadeh letter) is one of the poet's fixed images. زمستان ۱۳۶۸ is winter, so 1990 (+622). Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben. Correction (page 102 batch): the heading عاشقانه‌های ابری... ترانه‌های زمستانی... printed above this poem is a **sequence heading, not a title** — page 102 is printed '... از «عاشقانه‌های ابری»', '... from Cloudy Love Poems', which only makes sense if the phrase names a group of poems. The title fields, filled speculatively on import, are cleared; the poem is untitled like its neighbours and belongs to that sequence."
draft               = true

===persian===

خاکستریِ نگاهِ تو
آمیزه‌ای‌ست
از سرخِ عشق
و... سبزِ آرزو...
پیکر زیبای تو
از پنجه‌های پا
تا... چهارگوش خنده‌ها
برای هر که بخواهی
نگاهت...
نگاهت... برای من...
زمستان ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

The gray of your gaze
is a blend
of the red of love
and... the green of longing...
Your beautiful body,
from the toes of your feet
to... the square of your laughter,
for whomever you wish
your gaze...
your gaze... for me...
Winter 1368

===lantern===

The gray of your gaze
is a mixture
of the red of love
and... the green of longing...
Your beautiful body,
from the toes of your feet
to... the four corners of your laughter,
for anyone you please —
your gaze...
your gaze... for me...

Winter 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===

The opening is a painter's statement of fact before it is a compliment. Red and
green are complementaries: mixed, they cancel each other and go gray. So the gray of the gaze is
not a dulling of love and longing but exactly what you get when you have both at full strength —
which is worth holding beside the poem four pages earlier that ends "I love the grays, / the
grays..."

چهارگوش خنده‌ها, the four-cornered square of her laughter, is a fixed image of his: it turns up
as مربع خنده‌ها in the title poem and again in the letter to Alizadeh, always as a space a face
makes that something else can happen inside.

